      "claim": "Discharge of contractual duties can occur by agreement (e.g., novation, accord and satisfaction) or by operation of law.",
      "evidence": "General principles of contract law distinguish between voluntary discharge and involuntary discharge.",
      "weight": 0.8,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Novation is a discharge by agreement where a new contract replaces an old one, requiring the consent of all parties.",
      "evidence": "Definition of novation in contract law.",
      "weight": 0.7,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Accord and satisfaction is a discharge by agreement where parties agree to accept different performance than originally required.",
      "evidence": "Definition of accord and satisfaction in contract law.",
      "weight": 0.7,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Anticipatory repudiation is a breach of contract that gives the non-breaching party the right to sue, but does not automatically discharge duties by operation of law without further action (like rescission or acceptance of repudiation).",
      "evidence": "Legal effect of anticipatory repudiation.",
      "weight": 0.6,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Impossibility of performance is a doctrine where the law discharges a party's duties when performance becomes objectively impossible due to no fault of the party.",
      "evidence": "Doctrine of impossibility/impracticability in contract law.",
      "weight": 0.9,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "claim": "Among the options, only impossibility of performance is a circumstance that causes discharge by operation of law, while the others are primarily based on agreement or constitute a breach.",
      "evidence": "Comparison of the legal mechanisms for discharge.",
      "weight": 1.0,
      "depends_on": [2, 3, 4, 5]
    }
